// SWARM RABBIT HOLE — Wave 8 (Impl-J): W2A §1.5 T-PUSH/T-STORE — the
// escape rule's E-ESCAPE fail case.
//
// The reference's TARGET ('victim') is declared inside the inner
// `region {}` — the shorter region: it exits first. The holders
// ('keeper', the binding created in the outer region before the region
// opens, and 'box', the outer-region list) live in region 0. Storing a
// ref-to-'victim' into either holder violates the safety law
// ∀ρᵢ ∈ refs(v): ρᵢ ⊑ₗ reg(dst) (W2A §1.1; §4.1 P-I, the RI invariant:
// every live cell holding a ref to `a` has reg(a) ⊑ₗ reg(cell)) — the
// target's region is nested INSIDE the holder's region, so when the
// region exits the target dies before the holder: a dangling reference.
//
// The ref INTO a shorter region escapes; both store sites must reject
// it — the T-STORE binding assignment AND the T-PUSH list push.
// Compile-FAIL golden case (tests/golden.rs): [E-ESCAPE].
//
// The dual direction — a ref to OUTER data stored into an inner-region
// holder — is legal (the target outlives the holder) and is discharged
// by escape_ref_outer_ok.sn.

fn main() {
    int anchor = 1
    list<ref<int>> box = []
    ref<int> keeper = address(anchor)
    region {
        int victim = 42
        ref<int> inner = address(victim)
        keeper = inner
        box.push(address(victim))
    }
    print(value(keeper))
}
